/* Countdown quiz deck: bio. Generated from the revision notes — do not edit by hand. */
QZ.load("bio", {"chapters":{"1":["1","Characteristics and classification of living organisms"],"2":["2","Cells"],"3":["3","Movement into and out of cells"],"4":["4","Biological molecules"],"5":["5","Enzymes"],"6":["6","Plant nutrition"],"7":["7","Human nutrition"],"8":["8","Transport in plants"],"9":["9","Transport in animals"],"10":["10","Diseases and immunity"],"11":["11","Respiration and gas exchange"],"12":["12","Coordination and response"],"13":["13","Excretion and homeostasis"],"14":["14","Reproduction in plants"],"15":["15","Reproduction in humans"],"16":["16","Chromosomes, genes and proteins"],"17":["17","Variation and selection (and drugs)"],"18":["18","Organisms and their environment"],"19":["19","Human influences on ecosystems"],"20":["20","Biotechnology and genetic modification"]},"topics":{"bio-form":["Formulas","Write the formula"],"bio-facts":["Short facts","Answer"]},"cards":[["n-b1-fac-arthropods-exoskeleton","bio-facts",1,"Arthropods – key feature?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Arthropods – exoskeleton</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The exoskeleton is waterproof and supports the body; it lets arthropods live in very dry places without drying out. Because it is rigid, the legs must be <strong>jointed</strong> to move, and the animal must <strong>moult</strong> to grow.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-chlamydomonas-plant-like-c","bio-facts",1,"Chlamydomonas – what kind of cell?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chlamydomonas – plant-like cell</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Has cellulose cell wall + chloroplast; photosynthesises. Belongs to the <strong>protoctist</strong> kingdom (not plant kingdom) because it is a single cell.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-paramecium-animal-like-cel","bio-facts",1,"Paramecium – what kind of cell?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Paramecium – animal-like cell</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No cell wall or chloroplast; feeds on other organisms. Also a <strong>protoctist</strong>.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-seaweed-multicellular-prot","bio-facts",1,"Seaweed – what kind of protoctist?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Seaweed – multicellular protoctist</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most protoctists are unicellular, but seaweeds are multicellular protoctists (they have chloroplasts but are not true plants).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-species-is-the-smallest-gr","bio-facts",1,"Smallest and largest groups used in classification?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Species is the smallest group used in classification; kingdom is the largest.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The singular and plural of 'species' are the same word.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-viruses-do-not-belong-to-a","bio-facts",1,"Which kingdom do viruses belong to?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Viruses do not belong to any of the five kingdoms.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They are not made of cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-bacteria-are-the-oldest-gr","bio-facts",1,"Bacteria: how old is the group, and where do some live?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria are the oldest group of organisms (oldest fossils) and some live &gt;3 km underground.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Some bacteria photosynthesise.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-yeast-is-a-unicellular-fun","bio-facts",1,"Yeast and Penicillium: what are they?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Yeast is a <strong>unicellular fungus</strong>; <em>Penicillium</em> (a fungus) makes the antibiotic penicillin.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Some fungi cause disease, e.g. athlete's foot, ringworm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-only-mammals-have-hair-mam","bio-facts",1,"Features found only in mammals?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Only mammals have hair, mammary glands, a pinna, a diaphragm and a placenta.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Birds and mammals are the only warm-blooded (homeothermic) vertebrates and both have a 4-chambered heart.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-birds-are-the-only-animals","bio-facts",1,"The only animals with feathers?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Birds are the only animals with feathers.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Reptile eggs have soft leathery shells; bird eggs have hard shells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-amphibians-must-return-to","bio-facts",1,"What must amphibians do to breed?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amphibians must return to water to breed.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Their eggs have no shell and would dry out on land.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-insects-are-the-most-numer","bio-facts",1,"Most numerous group of animals on Earth?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Insects are the most numerous group of animals on Earth.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Success due to exoskeleton + tracheae (reduce water loss) and wings.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-when-writing-a-binomial-by","bio-facts",1,"Writing a binomial name by hand?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">When writing a binomial by hand, underline both words.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. Homo sapiens, written with Homo capitalised.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-for-magnification","bio-form",2,"Magnification","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">magnification = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">image size</span><span class=\"de\">actual size</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>actual size = image size ÷ magnification</b> · <b>image size = actual size × magnification</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">Both sizes in the same units. Magnification has no units: write × before it (e.g. ×1200). Measure the image with a ruler in mm.</p>",0,""],["n-b2-for-converting-units","bio-form",2,"Converting units","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">1 mm = 1000 µm</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">mm → µm: × 1000. µm → mm: ÷ 1000. Also 1 m = 1000 mm; 1 µm = 10⁻⁶ m. Give cell sizes in µm.</p>",1,""],["n-b2-fac-1-m-10-m-1-m-10-m","bio-facts",2,"1 µm in metres, and 1 m in µm?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">1 µm = 10⁻⁶ m; 1 m = 10⁶ µm</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Also <strong>1 mm = 1000 µm</strong> — this is the conversion used in the exam. A typical animal cell is 10–30 µm; a plant cell 40–100 µm; a bacterium 1–5 µm; a red blood cell ≈ 7 µm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-palisade-mesophyll-cell-le","bio-facts",2,"Palisade mesophyll cell – where is it found?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Palisade mesophyll cell – leaves of flowering plants</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Found just below the upper epidermis; tall cells packed with chloroplasts → main site of photosynthesis.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-bacteria-prokaryotic-cell","bio-facts",2,"Bacteria – what type of cell?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria – prokaryotic cell</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No nucleus (circular DNA), no mitochondria, no chloroplasts; has plasmids, ribosomes, cell wall.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-chloroplast-starch-grain","bio-facts",2,"Chloroplast – what storage product?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chloroplast – starch grain</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Glucose made in photosynthesis is converted to <strong>starch</strong> and stored as starch grains inside chloroplasts. Animal cells never contain starch.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-mitochondrion-aerobic-resp","bio-facts",2,"Mitochondrion – which process?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mitochondrion – aerobic respiration</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Number of mitochondria reflects energy demand (many in muscle, liver, sperm mid-piece, root hair cells).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-not-all-plant-cells-have-c","bio-facts",2,"Do all plant cells have chloroplasts?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Not all plant cells have chloroplasts.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Root cells and most epidermis cells have none; <strong>guard cells are the only epidermis cells with chloroplasts</strong>.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-not-all-cells-keep-all-the","bio-facts",2,"Do all cells keep all their parts?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Not all cells keep all their parts.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Red blood cells have no nucleus</strong> (more room for haemoglobin); <strong>xylem vessels have no cytoplasm or nucleus</strong> (dead, empty tubes); phloem sieve tubes have no nucleus.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-chloroplast-chlorophyll","bio-facts",2,"Chloroplast compared with chlorophyll?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chloroplast ≠ chlorophyll.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Chloroplast = the organelle; chlorophyll = the green pigment inside it.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-cell-walls-are-fully-perme","bio-facts",2,"Permeability of cell walls and cell membranes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cell walls are fully permeable; cell membranes are partially permeable.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So it is the <strong>membrane</strong> that controls entry, not the wall.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-bacteria-do-not-divide-by","bio-facts",2,"Do bacteria divide by mitosis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria do not divide by mitosis — they have no nucleus.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They reproduce by binary fission.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-cytoplasm-is-70-water","bio-facts",2,"Percentage of water in cytoplasm?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cytoplasm is ≈ 70% water.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Water is the solvent in which metabolic reactions take place.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-for-percentage-change-in-mass","bio-form",3,"Percentage change in mass (osmosis practical)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% change = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">final mass − initial mass</span><span class=\"de\">initial mass</span></span> × 100</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">A negative answer means the tissue lost water by osmosis. The same formula works for length.</p>",0,""],["n-b3-for-surface-area-to-volume-rat","bio-form",3,"Surface area to volume ratio","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">SA : V = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">surface area</span><span class=\"de\">volume</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">A smaller cube has a larger ratio, so substances diffuse into all of it faster.</p>",0,""],["n-b3-for-rate-of-diffusion","bio-form",3,"Rate of diffusion","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">rate = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">distance travelled</span><span class=\"de\">time</span></span></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-carrier-proteins-root-hair","bio-facts",3,"Carrier proteins – where, for example?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Carrier proteins – root hair cells, in cell membrane</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Protein carriers in the membrane of root hair cells pick up specific ions (e.g. nitrate, magnesium) and use energy to move them in against the gradient.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-concentrated-solution-wate","bio-facts",3,"Cell in a concentrated solution: what happens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Concentrated solution → water leaves → cell shrinks</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Because the solution has a lower water potential than the cell.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-pure-water-animal-cell-swe","bio-facts",3,"Animal cell in pure water: what happens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pure water → animal cell swells and bursts</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No cell wall to resist the pressure. Plant cells become turgid instead.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-low-water-potential-concen","bio-facts",3,"Low water potential = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Low water potential = concentrated solution</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Few 'free' water molecules.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-high-water-potential-dilut","bio-facts",3,"High water potential = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">High water potential = dilute solution</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pure water has the highest water potential.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-dialysis-tubing-partially","bio-facts",3,"Dialysis tubing: what does it let through?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dialysis tubing – partially permeable membrane: lets water through, not sugar</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Visking tubing is used to model the cell membrane / the gut wall.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-sugar-molecules-are-larger","bio-facts",3,"Sugar molecules compared with water molecules: size?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sugar molecules are larger than water molecules</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>That is why they cannot pass through the pores of a partially permeable membrane while water can.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-solutes-in-the-cytoplasm-d","bio-facts",3,"How do solutes move within the cytoplasm?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Solutes in the cytoplasm diffuse through the cytoplasm</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Diffusion also moves substances <em>within</em> a cell, e.g. O₂ to mitochondria.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-higher-temperature-more-ki","bio-facts",3,"Effect of a higher temperature on particle movement?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Higher temperature → more kinetic energy → faster movement</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So diffusion and osmosis are faster when warm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-diffusion-and-osmosis-are","bio-facts",3,"Which transport processes are passive, and which needs energy?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Diffusion and osmosis are passive; active transport needs energy from respiration.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Cells doing a lot of active transport (root hairs, villi epithelium, kidney tubule cells) have many mitochondria.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-sugars-and-mineral-ions-do","bio-facts",3,"Do sugars and mineral ions move by osmosis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sugars and mineral ions do <strong>not</strong> move by osmosis.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Osmosis refers only to water.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-surface-area-volume-ratio","bio-facts",3,"Surface area : volume ratio – small objects compared with large?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Surface area : volume ratio</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Small objects have a larger SA:V ratio, so diffusion can supply all of the inside quickly. Large organisms need special exchange surfaces (lungs, gills, villi, leaves) and transport systems.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-dna-is-made-of-2-strands-o","bio-facts",4,"Structure of DNA: strands and shape?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">DNA is made of 2 strands of nucleotides coiled to form a double helix</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Each nucleotide carries one base; the strands are held together by bonds between complementary bases.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-there-are-20-different-ami","bio-facts",4,"How many different amino acids are there?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">There are 20 different amino acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Their order (sequence) in the chain determines which protein is made and its shape.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-dcpip-blue-purple-liquid-b","bio-facts",4,"Vitamin C test: what happens to DCPIP?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">DCPIP (blue/purple liquid) becomes colourless when mixed with vitamin C</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The fewer drops of juice needed to decolourise a fixed volume, the more vitamin C.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-biuret-reagent-blue-change","bio-facts",4,"Biuret test for protein: colour change?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Biuret reagent (blue) changes to violet/purple with proteins</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No heating needed.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-fat-molecules-glycerol-fat","bio-facts",4,"A fat molecule is made of…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fat molecules = glycerol + fatty acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>One glycerol joined to three fatty acids.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-lipids-fats-oils","bio-facts",4,"Lipids = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lipids = fats + oils</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fats solid, oils liquid at room temperature.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-benedict-s-solution-blue-l","bio-facts",4,"Benedict&#x27;s test for reducing sugar: colour change?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Benedict's solution (blue liquid) turns orange-red when heated with reducing sugar</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Green/yellow = a little sugar; brick-red = lots.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-starch-iodine-solution-blu","bio-facts",4,"Starch with iodine solution: colour?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Starch + iodine solution → blue-black</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Iodine solution is orange-brown to start with.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-liver-helps-to-keep-the-co","bio-facts",4,"Liver – role in blood glucose?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Liver – helps to keep the concentration of glucose in the blood constant</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Stores excess glucose as glycogen (under insulin) and releases glucose (under glucagon) — Chapter 13.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-glucose-a-carbohydrate-c-h","bio-facts",4,"Formula of glucose?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucose (a carbohydrate) – C₆H₁₂O₆</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Glucose is the monomer of starch, glycogen and cellulose.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-sucrose-is-not-a-reducing","bio-facts",4,"Is sucrose a reducing sugar?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sucrose is <strong>not</strong> a reducing sugar.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It gives a negative Benedict's test unless first broken down (by acid or sucrase).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-haemoglobin-enzymes-antibo","bio-facts",4,"Haemoglobin, enzymes, antibodies, insulin and keratin are all…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Haemoglobin, enzymes, antibodies, insulin and keratin are all proteins.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Proteins are denatured by heat and extreme pH.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-fats-give-about-twice-as-m","bio-facts",4,"Energy per gram: fats compared with carbohydrates?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fats give about twice as much energy per gram as carbohydrates.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>≈ 39 kJ g⁻¹ vs ≈ 17 kJ g⁻¹.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-for-rate-of-an-enzyme-reaction","bio-form",5,"Rate of an enzyme reaction","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">rate = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">time taken</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Unit s⁻¹. Repeat each temperature or pH three times and calculate the mean.</p>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-optimum-ph-for-most-enzyme","bio-facts",5,"Optimum pH for most enzymes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Optimum pH for most enzymes ≈ pH 7</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exceptions: <strong>pepsin pH 2</strong> (stomach); <strong>pancreatic enzymes/trypsin pH ≈ 8</strong> (alkaline duodenum).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-optimum-temperature-for-hu","bio-facts",5,"Optimum temperature for human enzymes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Optimum temperature for human enzymes ≈ 37 °C</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Hot-spring bacteria have enzymes with optimum temperatures up to 80 °C</strong>; many plant enzymes have lower optima.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-carbohydrases-break-down-c","bio-facts",5,"Carbohydrases – break down what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Carbohydrases – break down carbohydrates</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Amylase (starch → maltose), maltase (maltose → glucose), sucrase (sucrose → glucose + fructose), lactase (lactose → glucose + galactose).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-sucrase-sucrose","bio-facts",5,"Sucrase – acts on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sucrase – sucrose</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Sucrose → glucose + fructose.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-maltase-maltose","bio-facts",5,"Maltase – acts on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Maltase – maltose</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Maltose → glucose (on membranes of small intestine epithelium).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-lipase-lipids","bio-facts",5,"Lipase – acts on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lipase – lipids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fats and oils → fatty acids + glycerol.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-catalase-h-o-water-oxygen","bio-facts",5,"Catalase – which reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Catalase – H₂O₂ → water + oxygen</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Found in almost all living cells (liver, potato, celery); removes toxic hydrogen peroxide; the fastest enzyme known. Easy to measure: collect/count O₂ bubbles or time floating paper discs.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-protease-protein-amino-aci","bio-facts",5,"Protease – which reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Protease – protein → amino acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. pepsin (stomach), trypsin (duodenum).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-amylase-starch-maltose-a-s","bio-facts",5,"Amylase – which reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amylase – starch → maltose (a sugar)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Salivary glands and pancreas.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-enzymes-are-not-used-up-an","bio-facts",5,"Are enzymes used up? How much is needed?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Enzymes are not used up and are only needed in small amounts.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They are reusable catalysts.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-all-enzymes-are-proteins","bio-facts",5,"What type of molecule are all enzymes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">All enzymes are proteins.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So they are denatured like other proteins by heat and extreme pH.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-leaf-heated-in-alcohol-eth","bio-facts",6,"Starch test on a leaf: why heat it in alcohol?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Leaf heated in alcohol (ethanol) — to remove chlorophyll</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Chlorophyll dissolves in hot alcohol; this decolourises the leaf so the iodine colour can be seen. Heat the alcohol in a <strong>water bath with the flame turned off</strong> — alcohol is flammable.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-boiling-the-leaf-breaks-do","bio-facts",6,"Starch test on a leaf: why boil it first?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Boiling the leaf — breaks down the cell membranes</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Kills the leaf, stops reactions, and lets iodine get into the cells to reach the starch. Then rinse in warm water to soften the brittle leaf before adding iodine.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-hydrogencarbonate-indicato","bio-facts",6,"Hydrogencarbonate indicator – what does it show?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Hydrogencarbonate indicator — effect of light and dark on gas exchange in an aquatic plant</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Light: purple (CO₂ used up by photosynthesis faster than produced by respiration). Dark: yellow (respiration only, CO₂ produced). No plant (control): stays red.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-potassium-hydroxide-or-sod","bio-facts",6,"Potassium hydroxide (or soda lime) – what does it do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Potassium hydroxide (or soda lime) — removes CO₂ from around a leaf</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Used to show CO₂ is needed. ⚠ Sodium <strong>hydrogencarbonate</strong> does the opposite — it <strong>adds/supplies</strong> CO₂ (used to raise CO₂ for pondweed). Hodder wrongly lists it as a CO₂ absorber.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-guard-cells-gas-exchange-b","bio-facts",6,"Guard cells – role?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Guard cells — gas exchange by diffusion through stomata</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pairs of guard cells open and close each stoma. They are the <strong>only epidermis cells with chloroplasts</strong>. CO₂ diffuses in, O₂ and water vapour diffuse out.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-spongy-mesophyll-air-space","bio-facts",6,"Spongy mesophyll – key feature?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Spongy mesophyll — air spaces</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Interconnecting air spaces give a large internal surface area for gas exchange.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-palisade-mesophyll-main-si","bio-facts",6,"Palisade mesophyll – role?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Palisade mesophyll — main site of photosynthesis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most chloroplasts; near the upper surface where light is brightest.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-epidermis-no-chlorophyll-e","bio-facts",6,"Leaf epidermis – does it photosynthesise?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Epidermis — no chlorophyll (except guard cells) — no photosynthesis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Transparent, so light passes through to the mesophyll; protects the leaf.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-0-04-of-air-is-co-it-reach","bio-facts",6,"Percentage of CO₂ in air, and how it reaches leaf cells?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">0.04% of air is CO₂; it reaches cells by diffusion</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So CO₂ is very often the limiting factor; glasshouses add extra CO₂ (paraffin burners, CO₂ cylinders).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-chlorophyll-contains-nitro","bio-facts",6,"Which elements does chlorophyll contain?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chlorophyll contains nitrogen and magnesium</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So plants need nitrate and magnesium ions to make chlorophyll.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-starch-not-glucose-is-test","bio-facts",6,"In leaves, do you test for starch or glucose?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Starch, not glucose, is tested for in leaves.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Glucose is quickly converted to starch for storage; starch present = photosynthesis has occurred.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-oxygen-produced-can-be-sho","bio-facts",6,"How can oxygen production be shown?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Oxygen produced can be shown with pondweed (Elodea/Cabomba).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Gas collected in an inverted test tube relights a glowing splint.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-light-intensity-1-distance","bio-facts",6,"Light intensity and distance from the lamp?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Light intensity ∝ 1 / distance²</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Moving a lamp from 10 cm to 20 cm reduces light intensity to ¼.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-bile-emulsifies-fat","bio-facts",7,"What does bile do to fat?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bile emulsifies fat</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Physical, not chemical digestion — bile contains no enzymes.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-stomach-wall-pepsin-ph-2","bio-facts",7,"Stomach wall – enzyme and pH?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Stomach wall — pepsin, pH 2</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The stomach also secretes mucus to protect its wall from acid and pepsin.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-small-intestine-maltase","bio-facts",7,"Small intestine lining – which enzyme?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Small intestine — maltase</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Maltase is not secreted into the lumen; it stays on the membranes (microvilli) of the epithelial cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-duodenum-ph-7-so-enzymes-w","bio-facts",7,"pH in the duodenum, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Duodenum pH &gt; 7 so enzymes work fast</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pancreatic juice (hydrogencarbonate) and bile neutralise stomach acid; pancreatic enzymes have optimum ≈ pH 8.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-pancreas-trypsin-protease","bio-facts",7,"Enzymes from the pancreas, and where they act?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pancreas → trypsin (protease), amylase, lipase — all act in the duodenum</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Also secretes hydrogencarbonate ions.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-walls-of-stomach-protease","bio-facts",7,"Walls of the stomach – which enzyme, acting where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Walls of stomach — protease (pepsin) acts in stomach</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-salivary-glands-amylase-ac","bio-facts",7,"Salivary glands – which enzyme, acting where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Salivary glands — amylase acts in mouth</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Starch digestion continues briefly in the bolus until stomach acid denatures the amylase.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-incisor-canine-premolar-mo","bio-facts",7,"The four types of teeth, front to back?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Incisor, canine, premolar, molar</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Adults: 8 incisors, 4 canines, 8 premolars, 12 molars (32 teeth).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-wisdom-teeth-third-molars","bio-facts",7,"Wisdom teeth = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Wisdom teeth = third molars</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Grow much later (late teens or after).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-dentine-quite-hard-rather","bio-facts",7,"Dentine – how hard?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dentine — quite hard, rather like bone</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Living tissue with channels containing cytoplasm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-enamel-hardest-substance-i","bio-facts",7,"Enamel – how hard, and what damages it?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Enamel — hardest substance in body but dissolved by acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Bacteria in plaque feed on sugar and make acid → tooth decay. Calcium and vitamin D (and fluoride) help healthy enamel.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-liver-secretes-bile-bile-s","bio-facts",7,"Where is bile made, and where is it stored?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Liver secretes bile; bile stored in gall bladder</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-pancreas-and-liver-are-par","bio-facts",7,"Are the pancreas and liver part of the alimentary canal?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pancreas and liver are part of the digestive system but not of the alimentary canal</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Food never passes through them — they are associated organs.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-rectum-stores-undigested-f","bio-facts",7,"Rectum – function?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Rectum stores undigested food (faeces)</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-large-intestine-colon-rect","bio-facts",7,"Large intestine = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Large intestine = colon + rectum</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Colon absorbs some water.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-ileum-digested-nutrients-a","bio-facts",7,"Ileum – what is absorbed there?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ileum — digested nutrients and water absorbed here</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most absorption happens in the ileum.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-duodenum-first-part-of-sma","bio-facts",7,"Duodenum – which part of the gut?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Duodenum — first part of small intestine</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pancreatic duct and bile duct empty into it.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-hcl-in-stomach-ph-2-optimu","bio-facts",7,"Hydrochloric acid in the stomach – pH and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">HCl in stomach — pH 2 (optimum for protease/pepsin)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>And kills microorganisms in food.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-goblet-cells-secrete-mucus","bio-facts",7,"Goblet cells – secrete what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Goblet cells secrete mucus</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Found along the <strong>whole</strong> alimentary canal (including the stomach) and in the trachea/bronchi.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-lumen-the-hole-in-the-cent","bio-facts",7,"Lumen = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lumen — the hole in the centre of the oesophagus (any tube) through which food passes</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Blood vessels and the trachea also have a lumen.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-saliva-water-mucus-amylase","bio-facts",7,"Saliva = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Saliva = water + mucus + amylase</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Mucus lubricates the bolus for swallowing.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-alimentary-canal-has-sphin","bio-facts",7,"What are sphincter muscles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alimentary canal has sphincter muscles</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Rings of muscle that contract to close the tube (e.g. top and bottom of stomach, anus).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-mucus-is-made-in-goblet-ce","bio-facts",7,"Where is mucus made?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mucus is made in goblet cells</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Lubricates food; protects lining.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-cytoplasm-is-a-solution-of","bio-facts",7,"Cytoplasm is a solution of…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cytoplasm is a solution of many substances in water</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><blockquote>\n<p>60% of the body is water.</p>\n</blockquote></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-calcium-deficiency-brittle","bio-facts",7,"Calcium deficiency – effects?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Calcium deficiency → brittle bones and teeth, poor blood clotting</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-lack-of-vitamin-c-scurvy-l","bio-facts",7,"Lack of vitamin C and lack of vitamin D cause…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lack of vitamin C → scurvy; lack of vitamin D → rickets</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-protein-builds-new-cells-g","bio-facts",7,"Protein – uses in the body?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Protein — builds new cells (growth), makes proteins such as haemoglobin, antibodies, insulin, enzymes</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-fat-insulator-heat-mechani","bio-facts",7,"Fat – uses in the body?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fat — insulator (heat), mechanical protection of organs, energy store</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-villi-and-microvilli-incre","bio-facts",7,"Villi and microvilli – what do they do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Villi and microvilli increase surface area of the small intestine, speeding up absorption</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Small intestine ≈ 5 m long — length also increases surface area and time for absorption.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-humans-cannot-digest-cellu","bio-facts",7,"Why does cellulose act as fibre?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Humans cannot digest cellulose — that is why it acts as fibre.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-food-stays-in-the-stomach","bio-facts",7,"How long does food stay in the stomach?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Food stays in the stomach about 1–2 hours.</p>",0,""],["n-b8-for-rate-of-water-uptake-potom","bio-form",8,"Rate of water uptake (potometer)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">rate = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">distance moved by the air bubble</span><span class=\"de\">time</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">This is approximately the rate of transpiration, because about 99% of the water taken up is lost. Volume taken up = distance × cross-sectional area of the capillary.</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-water-from-xylem-vessels-t","bio-facts",8,"How does water move from xylem to mesophyll cells?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Water from xylem vessels travels to mesophyll cells by osmosis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Then evaporates from their surfaces into the air spaces.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-vascular-bundles-xylem-ves","bio-facts",8,"Vascular bundles = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Vascular bundles = xylem vessels + phloem tubes</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-xylem-lignified-so-plants","bio-facts",8,"Xylem is lignified: what does that do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Xylem — lignified, so plants stay upright</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Lignin makes xylem strong; wood is mostly xylem.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-phloem-living-cells-transp","bio-facts",8,"Phloem – living or dead? Transports what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Phloem — living cells; transports sucrose and amino acids</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-xylem-dead-cells-transport","bio-facts",8,"Xylem – living or dead? Transports what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Xylem — dead cells; transports water and mineral ions</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-branching-shape-of-plants","bio-facts",8,"Branching shape of plants – advantage?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Branching shape of plants — large surface area</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most cells are near the surface, so gases can diffuse in/out of leaves directly; plants have no transport system for gases.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-water-does-not-move-up-xyl","bio-facts",8,"Does water move up the xylem by osmosis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Water does <strong>not</strong> move up xylem by osmosis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Xylem cells have no membranes; water moves by mass flow due to transpiration pull. (Hodder also mentions capillarity and root pressure — these are minor; the mark scheme wants <strong>transpiration pull + cohesion</strong>.)</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-minerals-are-absorbed-by-a","bio-facts",8,"How are minerals and water absorbed by roots?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Minerals are absorbed by active transport, water by osmosis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Both through root hair cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-stomata-are-mostly-on-the","bio-facts",8,"Where are most stomata on a dicot leaf, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Stomata are mostly on the lower surface of dicot leaves</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Cooler and shaded, so less evaporation.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-for-heart-rate-from-an-ecg","bio-form",9,"Heart rate from an ECG","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">heart rate (beats per minute) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">60</span><span class=\"de\">time between two R peaks (s)</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. peaks 0.8 s apart → 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75 beats per minute.</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-lymphocytes-produce-antibo","bio-facts",9,"Which cells produce antibodies?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lymphocytes produce antibodies</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-haemoglobin-is-a-protein-c","bio-facts",9,"What is haemoglobin?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Haemoglobin is a protein containing iron</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It combines reversibly with oxygen (oxyhaemoglobin) in the lungs and releases it in respiring tissues. Iron deficiency → anaemia.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-vein-lumen-is-wider-than-a","bio-facts",9,"Vein lumen compared with artery lumen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Vein lumen is wider than artery lumen</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Reduces resistance to the low-pressure flow.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-artery-wall-has-elastic-ti","bio-facts",9,"Artery wall – which tissue, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Artery wall has elastic tissue</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Stretches as each surge of blood passes, then recoils — this is the pulse; evens out pressure.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-pulse-rate-heart-rate","bio-facts",9,"Pulse rate = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pulse rate = heart rate</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-blockage-of-coronary-arter","bio-facts",9,"Blockage of a coronary artery causes…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Blockage of coronary artery → CHD</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-coronary-arteries-run-on-t","bio-facts",9,"Coronary arteries – where are they, and what do they supply?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Coronary arteries run on the outside of the heart and supply the heart muscle</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-heart-muscle-is-thick","bio-facts",9,"Heart muscle – thick or thin?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Heart muscle is thick</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Especially the left ventricle; cardiac muscle never tires.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-largest-artery-in-body-aor","bio-facts",9,"Largest artery in the body?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Largest artery in body — aorta</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-metabolically-active-tissu","bio-facts",9,"What do metabolically active tissues need a lot of?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Metabolically active tissues need a lot of oxygen</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. muscles, brain, liver — which is why a fast, high-pressure supply (double circulation) matters.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-right-side-of-heart-deoxyg","bio-facts",9,"Which side of the heart carries deoxygenated blood? (memory aid)","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Right side of heart = deoxygenated; left side = oxygenated (\"LORD\")</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-arteries-carry-blood-away","bio-facts",9,"Arteries carry blood which way? (memory aid)","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Arteries carry blood Away from the heart (\"AA\")</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Veins carry blood towards it.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-exercise-heart-rate-it-the","bio-facts",9,"Effect of exercise on heart rate, and afterwards?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Exercise ↑ heart rate; it then takes time to return to resting rate</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fitter people recover faster and have lower resting rates.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-coronaviruses-are-spherica","bio-facts",10,"Shape of coronaviruses?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Coronaviruses are spherical viruses (with protein spikes)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The spikes help the virus attach to human cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-covid-19-is-caused-by-the","bio-facts",10,"Cause of COVID-19?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">COVID-19 is caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2; spread by airborne droplets; controlled by masks, distancing, hand-washing, vaccines.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-measles-causes-a-skin-rash","bio-facts",10,"Symptoms of measles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Measles causes a skin rash and fever</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Caused by a virus spread in airborne droplets; complications include blindness and brain damage; controlled by vaccination (herd immunity).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-long-lasting-immunity-acti","bio-facts",10,"Long-lasting immunity = which type?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Long-lasting immunity = active immunity</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Because of memory cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-serious-transmissible-dise","bio-facts",10,"Cholera is caused by…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Serious transmissible disease — cholera (<em>Vibrio cholerae</em> bacteria)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Spread in water contaminated with faeces/raw sewage.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-untreated-sewage-raw-sewag","bio-facts",10,"Untreated sewage = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Untreated sewage = raw sewage</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Contains pathogens (cholera, polio, typhoid) and causes eutrophication (Ch 19).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-waste-liquid-sewage","bio-facts",10,"Waste liquid = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Waste liquid = sewage</p>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-methane-is-a-flammable-gas","bio-facts",10,"Methane – key property?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Methane is a flammable gas</p>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-decomposers-produce-methan","bio-facts",10,"Which organisms produce methane in landfill?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Decomposers produce methane gas</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>In landfill (anaerobic decay); pipes vent it or it is collected as fuel; methane is also a greenhouse gas.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-antibiotics-do-not-work-ag","bio-facts",10,"Do antibiotics work against viruses?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Antibiotics do not work against viruses.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>See Chapter 17 (drugs).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-houseflies-carry-pathogens","bio-facts",10,"How do houseflies spread pathogens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Houseflies carry pathogens on their feet and in their saliva.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They feed on faeces and then on our food.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-smallpox-was-eradicated-wo","bio-facts",10,"How and when was smallpox eradicated?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Smallpox was eradicated worldwide (1980) by vaccination.</p>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-lactic-acid-is-broken-down","bio-facts",11,"Where is lactic acid broken down?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lactic acid is broken down in the liver</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>By aerobic respiration (repaying the oxygen debt).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-glucose-lactic-acid-a-litt","bio-facts",11,"Anaerobic respiration in muscle: product?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucose → lactic acid (+ a little energy)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Anaerobic respiration in muscles.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-total-surface-area-of-all","bio-facts",11,"Total surface area of the alveoli?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Total surface area of all the alveoli in the lungs &gt; 100 m²</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>About the size of a tennis court half.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-alveolus-wall-is-one-cell","bio-facts",11,"Thickness of the alveolus wall?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alveolus wall is one cell thick</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So is the capillary wall — total diffusion distance &lt; 1 µm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-cilia-are-found-in-the-tra","bio-facts",11,"Where are cilia found in the gas exchange system?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cilia are found in the trachea and bronchi</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They sweep mucus upwards to the throat.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-limewater-turns-milky-clou","bio-facts",11,"Limewater test for carbon dioxide?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Limewater turns milky/cloudy with carbon dioxide</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Expired air turns limewater milky much faster than inspired air. Hydrogencarbonate indicator turns yellow with more CO₂.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-respiration-happens-in-all","bio-facts",11,"Where and when does respiration happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Respiration happens in all living cells all the time — in plants too.</p>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-the-brain-responds-to-co-c","bio-facts",11,"What does the brain detect to control the breathing rate?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The brain responds to CO₂ concentration (not O₂) to control breathing.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>CO₂ makes the blood slightly more acidic (lowers pH).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-plants-respond-to-stimuli","bio-facts",12,"How do plants respond to stimuli?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Plants respond to stimuli by growth</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Responses (tropisms) are slow compared with animals' nervous responses.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-nervous-control-acts-more","bio-facts",12,"Nervous and hormonal control: speed and duration?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Nervous control acts more quickly but for a shorter period</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Hormonal control is slower but longer-lasting.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-auxin-concentrates-on-the","bio-facts",12,"Where does auxin collect in a shoot lit from one side?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Auxin concentrates on the shady side of a shoot</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Those cells elongate more → bends towards light.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-suspensory-ligaments-conne","bio-facts",12,"What do the suspensory ligaments connect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Suspensory ligaments connect the lens to the ciliary muscles</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-suspensory-ligaments-tight","bio-facts",12,"Suspensory ligaments tight or slack: lens shape for distant and near vision?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Suspensory ligaments tight → lens thin (distant vision); slack → lens thick (near vision)</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-rods-dim-light-black-and-w","bio-facts",12,"Rods compared with cones?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Rods: dim light, black and white. Cones: colour, bright light</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-fovea-receptor-cells-cones","bio-facts",12,"Fovea – what is special about it?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fovea: receptor cells (cones) packed most closely</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Sharpest image.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-tears-contain-the-enzyme-l","bio-facts",12,"Which enzyme is in tears?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Tears contain the enzyme lysozyme</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Kills bacteria (a body defence).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-conjunctiva-protects-the-p","bio-facts",12,"Conjunctiva – function?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Conjunctiva protects the parts behind it</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Thin transparent membrane over the front of the eye; inflamed in conjunctivitis.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-myelin-insulates-the-nerve","bio-facts",12,"Myelin – what does it do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Myelin insulates the nerve fibre, so impulses are carried faster</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Up to ~100 m/s vs ~5 m/s without myelin.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-the-brain-and-spinal-cord","bio-facts",12,"Which neurones make up most of the brain and spinal cord?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The brain and spinal cord are made mostly of relay neurones.</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-most-refraction-occurs-at","bio-facts",12,"Where does most refraction in the eye happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Most refraction occurs at the cornea; the lens fine-tunes the focus.</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-glucagon-hormone-glycogen","bio-facts",12,"Glucagon compared with glycogen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucagon (hormone) ≠ glycogen (storage carbohydrate).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Spell both correctly.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-the-two-kidneys-filter-abo","bio-facts",13,"Water filtered by the kidneys each day compared with urine produced?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The two kidneys filter about 170 dm³ of water per day, but only about 1.5 dm³ of urine is produced in the same time</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So about 99% of the filtered water is reabsorbed.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-amino-acids-are-taken-to-t","bio-facts",13,"How do amino acids reach the liver?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amino acids are taken to the liver in the hepatic portal vein (HPV)</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-amino-acids-are-absorbed-i","bio-facts",13,"Where are amino acids absorbed?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amino acids are absorbed in the villi of the ileum</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-urea-is-made-from-excess-p","bio-facts",13,"Urea is made from…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Urea is made from excess protein (amino acids)</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-urethra-tube-from-bladder","bio-facts",13,"Urethra – from where to where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Urethra — tube from bladder to outside</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-ureter-tube-from-kidney-to","bio-facts",13,"Ureter – from where to where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ureter — tube from kidney to bladder</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Spelling matters: ureter (×2, from kidneys) vs urethra (×1, to outside).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-co-lowers-the-ph-of-cells","bio-facts",13,"Effect of CO₂ on the pH of cells and blood?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">CO₂ lowers the pH of cells and blood</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It dissolves to form a weak acid — so it is toxic and must be excreted by the lungs.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-faeces-are-not-excretion-e","bio-facts",13,"Are faeces excretion?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Faeces are not excretion (egestion), except for bile pigments in them.</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-glucose-in-urine-is-a-sign","bio-facts",13,"Glucose in urine is a sign of…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucose in urine is a sign of diabetes.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Normally all glucose is reabsorbed.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-body-temperature-is-kept-a","bio-facts",13,"Normal core body temperature?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Body temperature is kept at about 37 °C.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Enzymes' optimum; too high → denaturation; too low → slow reactions.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-eucryphia-hermaphrodite-re","bio-facts",14,"Eucryphia – type of flower and reproduction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Eucryphia — hermaphrodite — reproduces sexually</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Its flowers have both stamens and carpels.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-bacteria-hydra-potatoes-as","bio-facts",14,"Bacteria, Hydra, potatoes – which type of reproduction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria, <em>Hydra</em>, potatoes — asexual reproduction</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Bacteria by binary fission; <em>Hydra</em> by budding; potato by stem tubers.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-after-fertilisation-ovule","bio-facts",14,"After fertilisation: what do the ovule and ovary become, and what withers?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">After fertilisation: ovule → seed; ovary → fruit; petals, sepals and stamens wither.</p>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-a-seed-contains-an-embryo","bio-facts",14,"What does a seed contain?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A seed contains an embryo plant (radicle, plumule) and food stores (cotyledons/endosperm), surrounded by a testa (seed coat).</p>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-pollination-seed-dispersal","bio-facts",14,"Pollination compared with seed dispersal?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pollination ≠ seed dispersal.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Insects carrying pollen = pollination; animals carrying seeds/fruits = dispersal.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-aids-is-caused-by-hiv","bio-facts",15,"Cause of AIDS?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">AIDS is caused by HIV</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-if-the-egg-is-fertilised-p","bio-facts",15,"If the egg is fertilised: what happens to progesterone and FSH?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">If the egg is fertilised, progesterone levels stay high; FSH is inhibited (no new follicles develop)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So there is no menstruation and no ovulation during pregnancy.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-fsh-and-lh-are-made-contro","bio-facts",15,"Which gland makes FSH and LH?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">FSH and LH are made (controlled) by the pituitary gland</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Small endocrine gland at the base of the brain.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-sperm-swim-at-about-4-mm-p","bio-facts",15,"How fast do sperm swim?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sperm swim at about 4 mm per minute</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>About a million (hundreds of millions) are deposited, so a few reach the egg.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-semen-fluid-containing-spe","bio-facts",15,"Semen = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Semen = fluid containing sperm</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fluid from the prostate gland (and seminal vesicles) nourishes the sperm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-acrosome-digestive-enzymes","bio-facts",15,"Acrosome – contains?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Acrosome — digestive enzymes</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-cells-in-ovaries-and-teste","bio-facts",15,"How do cells in the ovaries and testes divide to form gametes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cells in ovaries and testes divide by meiosis to form gametes</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-egg-producing-organ-ovary","bio-facts",15,"Egg-producing organ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Egg-producing organ — ovary</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-other-name-for-oviduct-fal","bio-facts",15,"Other name for the oviduct?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Other name for oviduct — Fallopian tube</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-uterus-about-the-size-of-a","bio-facts",15,"Size of the uterus?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Uterus — about the size of a clenched fist</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It stretches enormously during pregnancy.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-sperm-are-stored-in-the-ep","bio-facts",15,"Where are sperm stored?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sperm are stored in the epididymis</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-fertilisation-happens-in-t","bio-facts",15,"Where do fertilisation and implantation happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fertilisation happens in the oviduct; implantation in the uterus.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-mother-s-and-fetus-s-blood","bio-facts",15,"Do the mother&#x27;s and the fetus&#x27;s blood mix?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mother's and fetus's blood never mix.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It would cause clotting/immune reactions (different blood groups) and high maternal blood pressure would damage the fetus.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-birth-the-muscular-uterus","bio-facts",15,"What happens during birth?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Birth: the muscular uterus wall contracts, cervix widens, baby pushed out through vagina; then the placenta (afterbirth) is expelled.</p>",0,""],["n-b16-for-probability-of-an-offsprin","bio-form",16,"Probability of an offspring's phenotype","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">probability = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">number of offspring with that outcome</span><span class=\"de\">total number of outcomes</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">A 3 : 1 ratio gives ¾ = 0.75 = 75%. A 1 : 1 ratio gives ½ = 0.5 = 50%.</p>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-bases-in-dna-a-t-g-c","bio-facts",16,"The four bases in DNA?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bases in DNA: A, T, G, C</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Adenine, thymine, guanine, cytosine (full names not required).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-sex-linked-genes-are-found","bio-facts",16,"Where are sex-linked genes found?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sex-linked genes are found only on the non-homologous part of the X (or Y) chromosome</p>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-abo-blood-groups-codominan","bio-facts",16,"ABO blood groups – which kind of inheritance?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">ABO blood groups — codominance (Iᴬ and Iᴮ)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Iᴼ is recessive.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-human-body-cell-46-chromos","bio-facts",16,"Chromosome number in a human body cell and in a gamete?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Human body cell: 46 chromosomes (23 pairs); gamete: 23.</p>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-three-bases-code-for-one-a","bio-facts",16,"How many bases code for one amino acid?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Three bases code for one amino acid.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So a protein of 51 amino acids needs a gene of at least 153 bases.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-a-red-blood-cell-has-no-nu","bio-facts",16,"Does a red blood cell have chromosomes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A red blood cell has no nucleus, so no chromosomes.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exam favourite.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-ratios-are-only-probabilit","bio-facts",16,"Are genetic ratios exact?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ratios are only probabilities.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>With small numbers of offspring the actual ratio may differ from 3:1 or 1:1.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-antibiotics-used-against-s","bio-facts",17,"Antibiotics used against Staphylococcus aureus?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Antibiotics used against <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>: penicillin, oxacillin, amoxicillin, methicillin</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>MRSA is resistant to methicillin (and the related penicillin-type antibiotics).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-natural-selection-example","bio-facts",17,"Classic example of natural selection (a moth)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Natural selection example — peppered moth (<em>Biston betularia</em>)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Dark form increased in polluted areas (camouflaged on soot); selection pressure = bird predation.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-hydrophytes-eichhornia-cra","bio-facts",17,"Examples of hydrophytes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Hydrophytes — <em>Eichhornia crassipes</em> (water hyacinth), <em>Cabomba</em></p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-xerophytes-sempervivum-ole","bio-facts",17,"Examples of xerophytes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Xerophytes — <em>Sempervivum</em>, oleander</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-continuous-variation-is-ca","bio-facts",17,"Continuous variation is caused by…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Continuous variation is caused by genes + environment</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-discontinuous-variation-is","bio-facts",17,"Discontinuous variation is caused by…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Discontinuous variation is caused by genes</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-discontinuous-variation-ex","bio-facts",17,"Example of discontinuous variation?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Discontinuous variation example: ABO blood groups (A, B, AB, O)</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-most-mutations-are-harmful","bio-facts",17,"Are most mutations harmful, neutral or beneficial?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Most mutations are harmful or neutral; a few are beneficial.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Mutations in gametes can be inherited; in body cells they may cause cancer.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b18-for-efficiency-of-energy-trans","bio-form",18,"Efficiency of energy transfer","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% efficiency = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">energy passed to the next trophic level</span><span class=\"de\">energy in the previous trophic level</span></span> × 100</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Usually about 10%. The rest is lost as heat from respiration, in movement, and in parts not eaten or digested.</p>",1,""],["n-b18-fac-about-90-of-energy-is-lost","bio-facts",18,"Energy lost and passed on at each trophic level?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">About 90% of energy is lost at each trophic level; ~10% passes on.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-decomposers-are-essential","bio-facts",18,"Decomposers are essential to which cycles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Decomposers are essential to both the carbon and nitrogen cycles.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-legumes-peas-beans-clover","bio-facts",18,"Legumes – what do their root nodules contain, and why are they used?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Legumes (peas, beans, clover) have root nodules containing nitrogen-fixing bacteria — farmers use them in crop rotation.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-food-chains-start-with-a-p","bio-facts",18,"Food chains: what do they start with, and which way do the arrows point?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Food chains start with a producer; arrows point to the eater.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-yeast-in-a-flask-shows-a-s","bio-facts",18,"Yeast in a flask: shape of the population curve?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Yeast in a flask shows a sigmoid curve followed by a death phase (food used up; ethanol toxic).</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-plastics-are-non-biodegrad","bio-facts",19,"Are plastics biodegradable?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Plastics are non-biodegradable</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-methane-is-produced-by-far","bio-facts",19,"Sources of methane from human activity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Methane is produced by farming activities (cattle, rice fields) and by decomposers in rubbish (landfill)</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-without-the-greenhouse-eff","bio-facts",19,"Earth&#x27;s temperature without the greenhouse effect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Without the greenhouse effect, Earth's average temperature would be about 33 °C lower than it is now</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The <em>natural</em> greenhouse effect is essential for life; the <em>enhanced</em> effect causes global warming.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-deforestation-increases-co","bio-facts",19,"Two ways deforestation increases CO₂?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Deforestation increases CO₂ in two ways: less photosynthesis + burning/decomposition of the trees.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-habitat-destruction-is-the","bio-facts",19,"Biggest threat to biodiversity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Habitat destruction is the biggest threat to biodiversity.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-seed-banks-keep-seeds-dry","bio-facts",19,"How do seed banks keep seeds?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Seed banks keep seeds dry and cold (about −20 °C) so they stay dormant for decades.</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-gm-bacteria-make-human-ins","bio-facts",20,"What do GM bacteria make (example)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">GM bacteria make human insulin</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Identical to human insulin; cheap, plentiful, no animal ethical/religious issues, fewer allergic reactions than pig/cow insulin.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-sweets-glucose-and-galacto","bio-facts",20,"Lactase and sweets: which sugars?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sweets — glucose and galactose from lactose-free milk (lactase)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Treating milk with lactase gives sweeter sugars used in sweets.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-milk-lactase-lactose-free","bio-facts",20,"Lactase and milk?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Milk — lactase → lactose-free milk</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-biological-washing-powders","bio-facts",20,"Which enzymes are in biological washing powders?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Biological washing powders contain enzymes (protease, lipase, amylase)</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-fruit-juices-pectinase-mor","bio-facts",20,"Which enzyme is used for fruit juices, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fruit juices — pectinase (more juice, clearer)</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-biofuel-ethanol-from-yeast","bio-facts",20,"Which biofuel does yeast fermentation make?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Biofuel — ethanol (from yeast fermentation)</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-yeast-co-bread-rises-and-e","bio-facts",20,"Yeast: products used in baking and brewing?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Yeast — CO₂ (bread rises) and ethanol (alcohol, biofuel)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Both by anaerobic respiration: glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-mycoprotein-quorn-is-made","bio-facts",20,"What is mycoprotein (Quorn)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mycoprotein (Quorn) is made from the fungus <em>Fusarium</em> grown in fermenters — a high-protein, low-fat meat substitute.</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-golden-rice-contains-beta","bio-facts",20,"What does Golden Rice contain?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Golden Rice contains beta-carotene (vitamin A precursor).</p>",0,""]],"defs":[["d-b1-excretion",1,"Excretion","<p>The removal of the waste products of metabolism and substances in excess of requirements.</p>","<p>Examples: carbon dioxide (lungs), urea, excess water and excess ions (kidneys). <em>Faeces/egestion are NOT excretion</em> — faeces are undigested food that was never made by the body's metabolism.</p>",0],["d-b1-binomial-system",1,"Binomial system","<p>An internationally agreed system in which the scientific name of an organism is made up of two parts showing the genus and the species.</p>","<p>Genus first with a <strong>capital</strong> letter, species second with a <strong>small</strong> letter; printed in <em>italics</em>, <strong>underlined</strong> when handwritten. e.g. <em>Homo sapiens</em>, <em>Panthera leo</em> (lion), <em>Equus caballus</em> (horse), <em>Equus asinus</em> (donkey) — same genus, different species. Because the name is the same in every language, scientists worldwide know exactly which organism is meant.</p>",0],["d-b1-characteristics-of-living-orga",1,"Characteristics of living organisms (MRS GREN)","<p><strong>Movement</strong> — an action by an organism or part of an organism causing a change of position or place.</p><p><strong>Respiration</strong> — the chemical reactions in cells that break down nutrient molecules and release energy for metabolism.</p><p><strong>Sensitivity</strong> — the ability to detect and respond to changes in the internal or external environment.</p><p><strong>Growth</strong> — a permanent increase in size and dry mass.</p><p><strong>Reproduction</strong> — the processes that make more of the same kind of organism.</p><p><strong>Excretion</strong> — the removal of the waste products of metabolism and substances in excess of requirements.</p><p><strong>Nutrition</strong> — the taking in of materials for energy, growth and development.</p>","<p>All living things show all seven:<br />\nLearn these <strong>word-for-word</strong> (they are the syllabus definitions). A non-living thing (car, fire) may show <em>some</em> of them but never all seven. If asked what you could see by watching an organism for a few minutes, give movement, sensitivity, nutrition — not growth, respiration or reproduction.</p>",0],["d-b1-species",1,"Species","<p>A group of organisms that can reproduce to produce fertile offspring.</p>","<p>Horse × donkey → mule, which is <strong>infertile</strong>, so horses and donkeys are different species.</p>",0],["d-b2-cells",2,"Cells","<p>The smallest units from which all organisms are made.</p><p>New cells are produced by division of existing cells.</p>","<p>Cells are the basic building blocks of life. New cells are made by mitosis in plants and animals, and by binary fission in bacteria. All cells contain cytoplasm, a cell membrane and DNA.</p>",0],["d-b2-tissues",2,"Tissues","<p>A group of cells with similar structures, working together to perform a shared function.</p>","<p>Examples: ciliated epithelium, muscle tissue, nervous tissue, blood (animals); palisade mesophyll, xylem, phloem, epidermis (plants).</p>",0],["d-b2-cell-levels-of-organisation",2,"Cell (levels of organisation)","<p><strong>Organ</strong> — a structure made of a group of <strong>tissues</strong> working together to perform a specific function (heart, stomach, brain, kidney, eye; leaf, stem, root, flower).</p><p><strong>Organ system</strong> — a group of <strong>organs</strong> with related functions working together to perform a body function (digestive, circulatory, nervous, respiratory, excretory, reproductive systems; plant shoot system).</p><p><strong>Organism</strong> — a complete living thing made of organ systems.</p>","<p><strong>Cell → tissue → organ → organ system → organism.</strong></p>",0],["d-b3-diffusion",3,"Diffusion","<p>The net movement of particles from a region of their higher concentration to a region of their lower concentration (i.e. down a concentration gradient), as a result of their random movement.</p>","<p>The <strong>energy for diffusion comes from the kinetic energy of random movement of molecules and ions</strong> — it is a passive process; the cell supplies no energy. <strong>Some substances move into and out of cells by diffusion through the cell membrane</strong> (e.g. O₂, CO₂, small solutes). <em>All three marking points are needed: net movement + high→low concentration + random movement.</em></p>",0],["d-b3-osmosis",3,"Osmosis","<p>The net movement of water molecules from a region of higher water potential (dilute solution) to a region of lower water potential (concentrated solution), through a partially permeable membrane.</p>","<p>Core: <strong>water diffuses through partially permeable membranes by osmosis</strong>; water moves into and out of cells by osmosis through the cell membrane. Osmosis is a special case of diffusion — <strong>only water</strong> moves, and it <strong>must</strong> cross a partially permeable membrane.</p>",0],["d-b3-active-transport",3,"Active transport","<p>The movement of particles through a cell membrane from a region of lower concentration to a region of higher concentration (i.e. against a concentration gradient), using energy from respiration.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Protein carriers</strong> in the membrane move the molecules or ions across, using energy from <strong>aerobic respiration</strong>. Importance: <strong>ion uptake by root hairs</strong> (e.g. <strong>nitrate ions</strong> absorbed from soil where they are less concentrated than in the root cell), glucose absorption in villi, reabsorption of glucose in kidney tubules. Anything that stops respiration (no oxygen, cyanide, low temperature) stops active transport.</p>",0],["d-b5-catalyst",5,"Catalyst","<p>A substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction and is not changed by the reaction.</p>","<p>Because it is unchanged, it can be used again and again.</p>",0],["d-b5-enzymes",5,"Enzymes","<p>Proteins that are involved in all metabolic reactions, where they function as biological catalysts.</p>","<p><strong>Importance</strong>: without enzymes, reactions at body temperature would be far too slow to sustain life — enzymes make them fast enough. They work in breakdown reactions (digestion: amylase, protease, lipase; catalase) and building-up reactions (making proteins, starch, DNA). Names usually end in <strong>-ase</strong>, named after the substrate (maltose → maltase).</p>",0],["d-b6-photosynthesis-and-its-equatio",6,"Photosynthesis and its equation","<p>The process by which plants synthesise carbohydrates from raw materials using energy from light.</p><p>Word equation: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen (in the presence of light and chlorophyll)</p><p>Balanced equation: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>","<p>(light, chlorophyll written over the arrow).<br />\n<strong>Chlorophyll</strong> is a <strong>green pigment found in chloroplasts</strong>; it <strong>transfers energy from light into energy in chemicals, for the synthesis of carbohydrates</strong>. Raw materials: CO₂ (diffuses in through stomata) and water (from roots via xylem). Products: glucose (used/stored) and oxygen (diffuses out as a by-product, or used in respiration).</p>",0],["d-b7-balanced-diet",7,"Balanced diet","<p>A diet that contains all the nutrients (carbohydrates, fats/oils, proteins, vitamins, mineral ions, fibre and water) in the correct proportions, and the right amount of energy, to maintain good health.</p>","<p>Energy needs depend on <strong>age, sex, activity level</strong> and pregnancy/breast-feeding.</p>",0],["d-b7-the-digestive-process-5-stages",7,"The digestive process (5 stages)","<p><strong>Ingestion</strong> — the taking of substances (food and drink) into the body (through the mouth).</p><p><strong>Digestion</strong> — the breakdown of food (physical and chemical).</p><p><strong>Absorption</strong> — the movement of nutrients from the intestines into the blood.</p><p><strong>Assimilation</strong> — the uptake and use of nutrients by cells.</p><p><strong>Egestion</strong> — the removal of undigested food from the body as faeces.</p>","",0],["d-b7-physical-digestion",7,"Physical digestion","<p>The breakdown of food into smaller pieces without chemical change to the food molecules.</p>","<p>Done by teeth (chewing), the stomach (churning) and bile (emulsifying). It <strong>increases the surface area of food for the action of enzymes</strong> in chemical digestion.</p>",0],["d-b7-chemical-digestion",7,"Chemical digestion","<p>The breakdown of large insoluble molecules into small soluble molecules.</p>","<p>(by enzymes). Its role: to <strong>produce small soluble molecules that can be absorbed</strong> into the blood.</p>",0],["d-b8-transpiration",8,"Transpiration","<p>The loss of water vapour from leaves.</p><p>Water evaporates from the surfaces of the mesophyll cells into the air spaces and then diffuses out of the leaves through the stomata as water vapour.</p>","<p>Uses of transpiration: pulls water and mineral ions up the plant; cools the leaf (evaporation); keeps cells turgid (supply of water). Most transpiration happens through stomata on the lower surface.</p>",0],["d-b8-translocation",8,"Translocation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The movement of sucrose and amino acids in the phloem from sources to sinks.</p>","<p>It can occur <strong>up or down</strong> the plant. Uses energy (phloem cells are living).</p>",1],["d-b8-source-and-sink",8,"Source and sink<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Source = a part of a plant that releases sucrose or amino acids.</p><p>Sink = a part of the plant that uses or stores sucrose or amino acids.</p>","<p>Sources: e.g. photosynthesising leaves, or a storage organ when its stores are being used. Sinks: e.g. growing roots and shoot tips, developing fruits and seeds, storage organs being filled.</p>",1],["d-b9-circulatory-system",9,"Circulatory system","<p>A system of blood vessels with a pump (the heart) and valves to ensure one-way flow of blood.</p>","<p>It transports oxygen, nutrients, CO₂, urea, hormones, antibodies and heat around the body.</p>",0],["d-b9-blood-clotting",9,"Blood clotting<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Clotting is the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin to form a mesh.</p>","<p>The mesh traps red blood cells to form a clot (sequence in the Describe &amp; explain section).</p>",1],["d-b10-pathogen",10,"Pathogen","<p>A disease-causing organism.</p>","<p>(microorganism). Types: <strong>viruses</strong> (flu, cold, measles, polio, HIV/AIDS, COVID-19), <strong>bacteria</strong> (cholera, TB, tetanus, whooping cough), <strong>protoctists</strong> (malaria, amoebic dysentery), <strong>fungi</strong> (athlete's foot, ringworm).</p>",0],["d-b10-transmissible-diseases",10,"Transmissible diseases","<p>A disease in which the pathogen can be passed from one host to another.</p>","<p>(Non-transmissible diseases — e.g. CHD, scurvy, diabetes — cannot be passed on.) <strong>Host</strong> = the organism in which a pathogen lives and reproduces.</p>",0],["d-b10-active-immunity",10,"Active immunity<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Defence against a pathogen by antibody production in the body.</p><p>Gained after an infection by a pathogen or by vaccination.</p>","<p><strong>Long-term</strong> because <strong>memory cells</strong> are made.</p>",1],["d-b10-passive-immunity",10,"Passive immunity<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A short-term defence against a pathogen by antibodies acquired from another individual, including across the placenta and in breast milk.</p>","<p>(or by injection of antibodies, e.g. anti-tetanus or anti-venom). Short-term because the antibodies are broken down and <strong>no memory cells are made</strong>.</p>",1],["d-b10-antibodies",10,"Antibodies<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Proteins that bind to antigens, leading to direct destruction of pathogens or marking of pathogens for destruction by phagocytes.</p><p>Specific antibodies have complementary shapes which fit specific antigens.</p>","<p>Ways they work: clump pathogens together (agglutination), burst them, neutralise their toxins, label them so phagocytes find and engulf them.</p>",1],["d-b10-cholera",10,"Cholera<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A disease caused by a bacterium (<em>Vibrio cholerae</em>), transmitted in contaminated water.</p>","<p>(and food) — water polluted with raw sewage. <strong>Mechanism:</strong> the bacterium multiplies in the <strong>small intestine</strong> and produces a <strong>toxin</strong> → the toxin causes <strong>secretion of chloride ions into the small intestine</strong> → this <strong>lowers the water potential</strong> of the gut contents → <strong>water moves by osmosis into the gut</strong> → <strong>diarrhoea</strong>, <strong>dehydration</strong> and <strong>loss of ions from the blood</strong> (can be fatal). <strong>Treatment:</strong> oral rehydration therapy (water + salts + glucose); antibiotics. <strong>Prevention:</strong> clean water, sewage treatment, hygiene, vaccine.</p>",1],["d-b11-aerobic-respiration",11,"Aerobic respiration","<p>The chemical reactions in cells that use oxygen to break down nutrient molecules to release energy.</p><p>Word equation: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water</p><p>Balanced equation: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>","<p>Happens in the <strong>mitochondria</strong>; releases a <strong>large amount</strong> of energy per glucose molecule.</p>",0],["d-b11-anaerobic-respiration",11,"Anaerobic respiration","<p>The chemical reactions in cells that break down nutrient molecules to release energy without using oxygen.</p><p>Yeast: glucose → alcohol (ethanol) + carbon dioxide</p><p>Yeast (balanced): C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p>Muscles: glucose → lactic acid</p>","<p>Happens in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>. <strong>Releases much less energy per glucose molecule than aerobic respiration</strong> (glucose is only partly broken down).<br />\n(no CO₂ made)</p>",0],["d-b12-reflex-action",12,"Reflex action","<p>A means of automatically and rapidly integrating and coordinating stimuli with the responses of effectors (muscles and glands).</p>","<p>Reflexes are <strong>automatic (involuntary)</strong>, <strong>rapid</strong>, and usually <strong>protective</strong> (e.g. withdrawal from pain, blinking, pupil reflex, coughing, knee-jerk).</p>",0],["d-b12-synapse",12,"Synapse","<p>A junction between two neurones.</p>","<p>Consisting of a tiny <strong>synaptic gap</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Structure</strong>: the end of the first neurone contains <strong>vesicles containing neurotransmitter molecules</strong>; then the <strong>synaptic gap</strong>; then the membrane of the next neurone with <strong>receptor proteins</strong>. <strong>Events</strong>: (a) <strong>an impulse stimulates the release of neurotransmitter molecules from vesicles into the synaptic gap</strong>; (b) <strong>the neurotransmitter molecules diffuse across the gap</strong>; (c) <strong>neurotransmitter molecules bind with receptor proteins on the next neurone</strong>; (d) <strong>an impulse is then stimulated in the next neurone</strong>. <strong>Synapses ensure that impulses travel in one direction only</strong> — neurotransmitter is only released on one side and receptors are only on the other.</p>",0],["d-b12-sense-organs",12,"Sense organs","<p>Groups of receptor cells responding to specific stimuli: light (eye), sound (ear), touch (skin), temperature (skin), chemicals (nose, tongue).</p>","<p>Receptors convert the stimulus into electrical impulses.</p>",0],["d-b12-hormone",12,"Hormone","<p>A chemical substance, produced by a gland and carried by the blood, which alters the activity of one or more specific target organs.</p>","",0],["d-b12-gravitropism-phototropism",12,"Gravitropism / phototropism","<p>Gravitropism — a response in which parts of a plant grow towards or away from gravity.</p><p>Phototropism — a response in which parts of a plant grow towards or away from the direction of the light source.</p>","<p>(roots positive, shoots negative). (shoots positive, roots negative). Both are examples of the <strong>chemical control of plant growth</strong> by auxin<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>.</p>",0],["d-b12-adrenaline",12,"Adrenaline","<p>The hormone secreted in 'fight or flight' situations.</p>","<p>Made by the adrenal glands; it increases breathing rate, heart rate and pupil diameter (and, Extended, raises blood glucose concentration).</p>",0],["d-b13-deamination",13,"Deamination<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The removal of the nitrogen-containing part of amino acids to form urea.</p>","<p>Happens in the <strong>liver</strong> to <strong>excess amino acids</strong> (they cannot be stored). The rest of the molecule is respired for energy or converted to carbohydrate/fat.</p>",1],["d-b13-homeostasis",13,"Homeostasis","<p>The maintenance of a constant internal environment.</p>","<p>Internal environment = the blood and tissue fluid around cells. Factors kept constant: <strong>body temperature</strong>, <strong>blood glucose concentration</strong>, <strong>water content (water potential)</strong> of blood, CO₂ concentration/pH. Cells (and their enzymes) work best in constant conditions.</p>",0],["d-b14-asexual-reproduction",14,"Asexual reproduction","<p>A process resulting in the production of genetically identical offspring from one parent.</p>","<p>No gametes, no fertilisation; cells divide by <strong>mitosis</strong>. Offspring = a <strong>clone</strong>. Examples: bacteria (binary fission), fungi (spores, e.g. <em>Mucor</em>), yeast (budding), <em>Hydra</em> (budding), potato (stem tubers), onion/daffodil (bulbs), strawberry (runners), ginger (rhizomes), cuttings and tissue culture.</p>",0],["d-b14-sexual-reproduction",14,"Sexual reproduction","<p>A process involving the fusion of the nuclei of two gametes to form a zygote and the production of offspring that are genetically different from each other.</p>","<p>Gametes are made by <strong>meiosis</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b14-fertilisation",14,"Fertilisation","<p>The fusion of the nuclei of gametes.</p>","<p>In flowering plants, <strong>fertilisation occurs when a pollen nucleus fuses with a nucleus in an ovule</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b14-pollination",14,"Pollination","<p>The transfer of pollen grains from an anther to a stigma.</p><p>Self-pollination: transfer of pollen from the anther of a flower to the stigma of the same flower or a different flower on the same plant.</p><p>Cross-pollination: transfer of pollen from the anther of a flower to the stigma of a flower on a different plant of the same species.</p>","<p>By <strong>insects</strong> (or other animals) or <strong>wind</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b14-haploid-and-diploid",14,"Haploid and diploid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Nuclei of gametes are haploid.</p><p>The nucleus of a zygote is diploid.</p>","<p>Haploid = one set of chromosomes (<em>n</em>); diploid = two sets (2<em>n</em>). Fertilisation restores the diploid number.</p>",1],["d-b15-fertilisation",15,"Fertilisation","<p>The fusion of the nuclei from a male gamete (sperm) and a female gamete (egg cell).</p>","<p>In the <strong>oviduct</strong>. Once one sperm enters, the <strong>jelly coat changes</strong> to stop any more sperm entering. The result is a <strong>zygote</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b15-sexually-transmitted-infection",15,"Sexually transmitted infection (STI)","<p>An infection that is transmitted through sexual contact.</p>","<p>(via body fluids). Examples: <strong>HIV</strong> (a virus — <strong>a pathogen that causes an STI</strong>), gonorrhoea, syphilis, chlamydia (bacteria).</p>",0],["d-b16-gene",16,"Gene","<p>A length of DNA that codes for a protein.</p>","<p>Each gene has a fixed position (locus) on a chromosome.</p>",0],["d-b16-allele",16,"Allele","<p>An alternative form of a gene.</p>","<p>e.g. the gene for pea height has alleles <strong>T</strong> (tall) and <strong>t</strong> (dwarf). A diploid cell has two alleles of each gene (one on each chromosome of a pair).</p>",0],["d-b16-reduction-division-meiosis",16,"Reduction division (meiosis)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Meiosis is a reduction division in which the chromosome number is halved from diploid to haploid, resulting in genetically different cells.</p>","<p>(4 haploid nuclei from 1 diploid). <strong>Meiosis is involved in the production of gametes</strong>. (Details of the stages are not required.)</p>",1],["d-b16-codominance",16,"Codominance<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A situation in which both alleles in a heterozygous organism contribute to the phenotype.</p>","<p>(neither is dominant). Written with a capital letter for the gene and superscripts for alleles, e.g. <strong>Iᴬ Iᴮ</strong> → blood group <strong>AB</strong>; flower colour <strong>Cᴿ Cᵂ</strong>. Heterozygotes have a third, distinct phenotype.</p>",1],["d-b16-inheritance",16,"Inheritance","<p>The transmission of genetic information from generation to generation.</p>","",0],["d-b16-sex-linkage",16,"Sex linkage<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A sex-linked characteristic is a feature in which the gene responsible is located on a sex chromosome (usually the X), which makes the characteristic more common in one sex than in the other.</p>","<p>Males have only one X, so a single recessive allele is always expressed in a male (no second X to mask it).</p>",1],["d-b16-stem-cells",16,"Stem cells<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Unspecialised cells that divide by mitosis to produce daughter cells that can become specialised for specific functions.</p>","<p>e.g. embryonic stem cells (can become any cell type), adult stem cells in <strong>bone marrow</strong> (→ blood cells), skin.</p>",1],["d-b16-haploid-diploid-nucleus",16,"Haploid / diploid nucleus<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Haploid nucleus — contains a single set of chromosomes.</p><p>Diploid nucleus — contains two sets of chromosomes.</p>","<p>Human gametes have 23 chromosomes (haploid). In a diploid cell there is a <strong>pair of each type</strong> of chromosome; <strong>human body cells: 23 pairs = 46</strong>.</p>",1],["d-b16-mitosis",16,"Mitosis<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Nuclear division giving rise to genetically identical cells.</p>","<p><strong>Exact replication (copying) of the chromosomes occurs before mitosis</strong>; <strong>during mitosis the copies of chromosomes separate, maintaining the chromosome number in each daughter cell</strong>. <strong>Roles:</strong> <strong>growth</strong>, <strong>repair of damaged tissues</strong>, <strong>replacement of cells</strong> (worn-out skin, blood), <strong>asexual reproduction</strong>. (Stages not required.)</p>",1],["d-b16-genotype-phenotype",16,"Genotype / phenotype","<p>Genotype — the genetic make-up of an organism in terms of the alleles present.</p><p>Phenotype — the observable features of an organism.</p>","<p>e.g. genotype <strong>Tt</strong> → phenotype <strong>tall</strong>. The phenotype is the result of the genotype <strong>and</strong> the environment.</p>",0],["d-b16-homozygous-heterozygous",16,"Homozygous / heterozygous","<p>Homozygous — having two identical alleles of a particular gene.</p><p>Heterozygous — having two different alleles of a particular gene.</p>","<p>Homozygous: <strong>TT</strong> or <strong>tt</strong>. Heterozygous: <strong>Tt</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b16-dominant-recessive",16,"Dominant / recessive","<p>Dominant allele — an allele that is expressed if it is present in the genotype.</p><p>Recessive allele — an allele that is only expressed when there is no dominant allele of the gene present in the genotype.</p>","<p>Dominant allele = <strong>capital</strong> letter (T); recessive = <strong>small</strong> letter (t) — a recessive characteristic only shows when the genotype is homozygous recessive (tt).</p>",0],["d-b17-variation",17,"Variation","<p>Differences between individuals of the same species.</p>","<p>Two types: <strong>continuous</strong> and <strong>discontinuous</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b17-population",17,"Population","<p>A group of organisms of one species, living in the same area, at the same time.</p>","<p>Variation exists within every population.</p>",0],["d-b17-adaptive-feature",17,"Adaptive feature","<p>An inherited feature that helps an organism to survive and reproduce in its environment.</p>","<p>You must be able to interpret images of a species and describe its adaptive features — state the feature <strong>and</strong> how it helps (e.g. \"thick white fur — insulation and camouflage in snow\").</p>",0],["d-b17-adaptation",17,"Adaptation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The process, resulting from natural selection, by which populations become more suited to their environment over many generations.</p>","<p>(Different from 'adaptive feature', which is the feature itself.)</p>",1],["d-b17-drug",17,"Drug","<p>Any substance taken into the body that modifies or affects chemical reactions in the body.</p>","<p>Medicinal drugs (antibiotics, painkillers) and recreational drugs (caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, heroin).</p>",0],["d-b17-continuous-variation",17,"Continuous variation","<p>Results in a range of phenotypes between two extremes.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>body length/height, body mass</strong>, leaf length, hand span. Caused by <strong>genes (many) and the environment</strong>. Shown by a <strong>histogram</strong> (bars touching; normal / bell-shaped distribution).</p>",0],["d-b17-discontinuous-variation",17,"Discontinuous variation","<p>Results in a limited number of phenotypes with no intermediates.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>ABO blood groups, seed shape in peas (round/wrinkled), seed colour in peas (yellow/green)</strong>, tongue rolling. Usually caused by <strong>genes only</strong> (one or two genes). Shown by a <strong>bar chart</strong> (bars separate).</p>",0],["d-b17-mutation-and-gene-mutation",17,"Mutation and gene mutation","<p>Mutation is genetic change.</p><p>Gene mutation <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> is a random change in the base sequence of DNA.</p>","<p>Mutation is the way new alleles are formed; ionising radiation and some chemicals increase the rate of mutation.</p>",0],["d-b18-community",18,"Community","<p>All of the populations of different species in an ecosystem.</p>","",0],["d-b18-ecosystem",18,"Ecosystem","<p>A unit containing the community of organisms and their environment, interacting together.</p>","<p>(e.g. a lake, a forest, a coral reef). Includes the non-living parts: water, soil, light, temperature, minerals, oxygen.</p>",0],["d-b18-food-chain",18,"Food chain","<p>A diagram showing the transfer of energy from one organism to the next, beginning with a producer.</p>","<p>Arrows point in the direction of energy flow (from the eaten to the eater). e.g. grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → eagle. Don't include the Sun.</p>",0],["d-b18-food-web",18,"Food web","<p>A network of interconnected food chains.</p>","<p>More realistic, because most animals eat more than one type of food and are eaten by more than one predator.</p>",0],["d-b18-producer",18,"Producer","<p>An organism that makes its own organic nutrients, usually using energy from sunlight, through photosynthesis.</p>","<p>Green <strong>plants</strong> (and algae, some bacteria). First trophic level.</p>",0],["d-b18-consumer",18,"Consumer","<p>An organism that gets its energy by feeding on other organisms.</p><p>Herbivore — an animal that gets its energy by eating plants.</p><p>Carnivore — an animal that gets its energy by eating other animals.</p>","<p>Classed as <strong>primary (1°), secondary (2°), tertiary (3°) and quaternary (4°)</strong> according to position in the food chain. Herbivores are <strong>primary consumers</strong>; carnivores are <strong>secondary or higher</strong> consumers. Omnivores eat both.</p>",0],["d-b18-decomposer",18,"Decomposer","<p>An organism that gets its energy from dead or waste organic material.</p>","<p>Mainly <strong>bacteria and fungi</strong>. They secrete enzymes onto dead material, absorb the products, and <strong>release CO₂ and mineral ions</strong> (e.g. ammonium) back to the environment — essential for nutrient cycles.</p>",0],["d-b18-trophic-level",18,"Trophic level","<p>The position of an organism in a food chain, food web or ecological pyramid.</p>","<p><strong>producers</strong> (1st), <strong>primary consumers</strong> (2nd), <strong>secondary consumers</strong> (3rd), <strong>tertiary consumers</strong> (4th), <strong>quaternary consumers</strong> (5th). One organism may be at more than one trophic level in a web.</p>",0],["d-b19-biodiversity",19,"Biodiversity","<p>The number of different species that live in an area.</p>","<p>(High in rainforests and coral reefs.)</p>",0],["d-b19-sustainable-resource",19,"Sustainable resource","<p>A resource which is produced as rapidly as it is removed from the environment so that it does not run out.</p>","<p><strong>Some resources can be conserved and managed sustainably — limited to forests and fish stocks</strong>. (Fossil fuels are non-renewable — not sustainable.)</p>",0],["d-b20-genetic-modification-gm",20,"Genetic modification (GM)","<p>Changing the genetic material of an organism by removing, changing or inserting individual genes.</p>","<p>(often from another species). Also called genetic engineering.</p>",0]]});
